--- title Refinements the suite reaches for
--- about Each entry pins one refinement of one native. They are gathered
--- about here rather than filed under the native because what is being
--- about tested is the same thing every time: that asking for a named
--- about refinement changes the answer in the way R3 says it does.

--- id refinements/ajoin-drops-none-by-default
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the fact /all exists to undo, so it is pinned first.
--- code
ajoin ["a" none "b"]
--- result
"ab"

--- id refinements/ajoin-all-keeps-none
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
ajoin/all ["a" none "b"]
--- result
"anoneb"

--- id refinements/ajoin-with-puts-a-separator-between
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Between, not after: three items give two separators.
--- code
ajoin/with ["a" "b" "c"] "-"
--- result
"a-b-c"

--- id refinements/append-part-takes-only-the-first-few
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
append/part [1 2] [3 4 5] 2
--- result
[1 2 3 4]

--- id refinements/append-part-on-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
append/part "ab" "cde" 2
--- result
"abcd"

--- id refinements/remove-part-drops-several
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And answers what is left from the position, not the whole
--- note series, which is easy to get the wrong way round.
--- code
remove/part [1 2 3 4] 2
--- result
[3 4]

--- id refinements/take-all-takes-the-lot
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
take/all [1 2 3]
--- result
[1 2 3]

--- id refinements/select-case-minds-the-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Without /case the first key wins, so this is the boundary
--- note between finding "A" and finding "a".
--- code
select/case ["A" 1 "a" 2] "a"
--- result
2

--- id refinements/select-skip-treats-the-block-as-records
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Three-wide records, so the value that follows key 3 is 4.
--- code
select/skip [1 2 3 4] 3 2
--- result
4

--- id refinements/switch-case-minds-the-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
switch/case "a" ["A" [1] "a" [2]]
--- result
2

--- id refinements/trim-head-only-trims-the-front
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
trim/head "  ab  "
--- result
"ab  "

--- id refinements/trim-tail-only-trims-the-back
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
trim/tail "  ab  "
--- result
"  ab"

--- id refinements/parse-case-refuses-the-wrong-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note PARSE ignores case by default, so this is the one that shows
--- note /case doing something.
--- code
parse/case "AB" ["ab"]
--- result
#(false)

--- id refinements/parse-case-accepts-the-right-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse/case "AB" ["AB"]
--- result
#(true)

--- id refinements/reduce-into-answers-the-position-after-what-it-put
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Not the target and not what was reduced. Inserting 2 at the
--- note head of [9] leaves the answer looking at [9], which reads as
--- note though nothing happened until you look at the target again.
--- code
reduce/into [1 + 1] [9]
--- result
[9]

--- id refinements/reduce-into-really-does-fill-the-target
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
target: [9]
reduce/into [1 + 1] target
target
--- result
[2 9]
